```javascript
function createCounter(start = 0) {
  let value = start; // private — no property on the returned object holds it

  return {
    increment() { value += 1; return value; },
    decrement() { value -= 1; return value; },
    getValue() { return value; },
  };
}

const counter = createCounter();
counter.increment(); // 1
counter.increment(); // 2
counter.getValue();  // 2
counter.value;       // undefined — no such property
```

The mechanism: `value` lives in `createCounter`'s variable environment. Normally that environment would be discarded the moment `createCounter` returns, but all three returned methods hold a live reference into it, so the engine keeps it reachable. Each method reads and writes the **same** `value`, which is why `increment` followed by `getValue` sees the updated number — they're not three isolated functions, they're three views onto one shared closure.

Calling `createCounter()` again creates a **completely separate** `value` — that's what makes this pattern safe to reuse as a factory, unlike a single shared module-level variable would be.

I'd mention the modern alternative too: a class with a `#value` private field gives the same encapsulation with different syntax — the interview value here is showing you understand *why* the closure achieves privacy, not that it's the only way to get it.

**Testing:** whether you can produce working private-state code live, not just describe closures in the abstract.

It breaks because `var` creates **one** binding for `i` across the entire loop, so all three click handlers close over the same variable. By the time any handler fires — after the loop has finished attaching all three listeners — `i` holds its final value, and every handler reads that one shared number.

The cleanest fix is swapping `var` for `let`:

```javascript
const buttons = document.querySelectorAll("button");
for (let i = 0; i < buttons.length; i++) {
  buttons[i].addEventListener("click", () => console.log(i));
}
```

`let` gives the loop a **fresh binding per iteration**, so each handler's closure captures its own copy of `i` at the time it was attached, not a shared mutable variable.

If I couldn't touch the loop's variable declaration — say it's forced to stay `var` for compatibility — I'd pass the index in explicitly instead of relying on the closure:

```javascript
buttons.forEach((btn, i) => btn.addEventListener("click", () => console.log(i)));
```

`forEach`'s callback parameter `i` is a fresh function parameter on every call, which sidesteps the shared-variable problem entirely — it's the same fix in spirit as the IIFE pattern, just built into the array method.

**Testing:** whether you can both diagnose the shared-binding root cause and produce more than one working fix.
